= A
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Martingales Through Measure Theory}

= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A <measurable space> is a set $E$ together with a <sigma-algebra> $\mathcal E$ of its subsets: it contains $E$ and is closed under complements relative to $E$ and countable unions. A <measure> is a map $\mu:\mathcal E\to[0,\infty]$ with $\mu(\varnothing)=0$ and <countable additivity> on disjoint measurable sets:
$$
\mu\left(\bigcup_jA_j\right)=\sum_j\mu(A_j).
$$
A finite measure additionally satisfies $\mu(E)<\infty$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $\varepsilon>0$, define the measurable decreasing tail events
$$
A_n(\varepsilon)=\bigcup_{m\geq n}\{|f_m-f|>\varepsilon\}
=\left\{\sup_{m\geq n}|f_m-f|>\varepsilon\right\}.
$$
If $f_n\to f$ almost everywhere, every point outside its null exceptional set eventually leaves each such tail event, so $\mu(\bigcap_nA_n(\varepsilon))=0$. <Continuity from above of a measure> applies because $\mu(E)<\infty$, giving $\mu(A_n(\varepsilon))\to0$.

Conversely, assume these measures tend to zero. For each positive integer $k$, the intersection $\bigcap_nA_n(1/k)$ has measure zero. Outside the union of these countably many null sets, every $k$ has an index after which $|f_m-f|\leq1/k$ for all later $m$. Hence $f_m\to f$ there. This proves the desired equivalence. The argument uses all positive thresholds; replacing the intersection of tail events by a single strict limsup inequality at the same threshold would mishandle equality cases.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <triangle inequality> gives the event inclusion
$$
\{|f_n-f_m|>\varepsilon\}\subseteq
\{|f_n-f|>\varepsilon/2\}\cup\{|f_m-f|>\varepsilon/2\}.
$$
<Countable subadditivity of a measure> bounds its measure by the sum of the two terms on the right. Both tend to zero as $m,n\to\infty$ by <convergence in measure>. Thus **convergence in measure implies the Cauchy property in measure**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The Cauchy assumption lets us choose $n_k$ recursively, increasing strictly, so that $\mu(|f_n-f_m|>2^{-k})<2^{-k}$ for all $m,n\geq n_k$. In particular the measurable events
$$
E_k=\{|f_{n_{k+1}}-f_{n_k}|>2^{-k}\}
$$
have summable measures. The measure of $\bigcup_{k\geq K}E_k$ is at most $\sum_{k\geq K}2^{-k}\to0$, so $\limsup E_k$ is a null set. Outside it, the successive differences are eventually bounded by a summable geometric series. Hence $f_{n_k}$ is pointwise Cauchy and has a finite real limit.

Define $f$ to be that limit wherever the sequence converges, and zero elsewhere. The convergence set is measurable by the countable Cauchy criterion, and the limit is measurable as a limit of measurable functions on that set. Thus there is no need to assume the measure is complete. Part (a)(ii), or the corresponding finite-measure implication, gives $f_{n_k}\to f$ in measure.

For the full sequence use
$$
\mu(|f_n-f|>\varepsilon)\leq
\mu(|f_n-f_{n_k}|>\varepsilon/2)+\mu(|f_{n_k}-f|>\varepsilon/2).
$$
Choose a sufficiently late subsequence index so the second term is small; the Cauchy property then bounds the first uniformly for all large $n$. Therefore **$f_n\to f$ in measure**. This proves <completeness of convergence in measure on a finite measure space>.

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

<Kolmogorov zero-one law> states that every event in the tail sigma-algebra of an independent sequence has probability zero or one. Removing any fixed number of summands changes $S_n/n$ by a finite random constant divided by $n$, so does not change its extended-real limsup. Therefore $\overline X$ is measurable with respect to the tail sigma-algebra. Each event $\{\overline X\leq q\}$ for rational $q$ has probability zero or one. Monotonicity of these events forces the distribution to be concentrated at one extended-real value, allowing either infinity.

The <strong law of large numbers> says that for independent identically distributed variables with $\mathbb E|X_1|<\infty$,
$$
\boxed{S_n/n\longrightarrow\mathbb E X_1\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Pointwise, the positive integers $n$ with $Z\geq n$ are exactly $1,\ldots,\lfloor Z\rfloor$. Thus $Y=\lfloor Z\rfloor=\sum_{n\geq1}\mathbf1_{\{Z\geq n\}}$. The <monotone convergence theorem> gives $\mathbb EY=\sum_{n\geq1}\mathbb P(Z\geq n)$. Since $Y\leq Z<Y+1$,
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\geq1}\mathbb P(Z\geq n)\leq\mathbb EZ\leq1+\sum_{n\geq1}\mathbb P(Z\geq n).}
$$
These inequalities also hold for infinite expectations. This is the <tail-sum formula> comparison for a nonnegative real variable.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For any $\lambda>0$, the variable $Z=|X_1|/\lambda$ has infinite expectation. Part (a)(ii) therefore forces $\sum_n\mathbb P(|X_1|\geq\lambda n)=\infty$. Identical distributions give
$$
\boxed{\sum_n\mathbb P(|X_n|\geq\lambda n)=\infty.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For each positive integer $m$, the events $\{|X_n|\geq mn\}$ are independent and have divergent total probability by part (i). The <Borel-Cantelli second lemma> makes them occur infinitely often. Taking the countable intersection over $m$ gives
$$
\boxed{\limsup_n|X_n|/n=\infty\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
If $\limsup_n|S_n|/n$ were finite along any such sample path, then for some finite path-dependent $C$, $|S_n|\leq Cn$ eventually. But $X_n=S_n-S_{n-1}$ would give $|X_n|/n\leq C+C(n-1)/n\leq2C$ eventually, a contradiction. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\limsup_n|S_n|/n=\infty\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
This is the <divergence of normalized iid partial sums without a first absolute moment>.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <Borel-Cantelli second lemma> says that independent events whose probabilities have divergent sum occur infinitely often almost surely. The events here are independent and have probabilities $\min\{1,\lambda/n\}$. Their sum diverges by the harmonic series, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(U_n\leq\lambda/n\text{ infinitely often})=1\quad(\lambda>0).}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The printed update needs a one-step index correction to match its filtration: the draw for the transition from $n$ to $n+1$ must be the fresh variable $U_{n+1}$. Also the trivial initial sigma-algebra is $\{\varnothing,\Omega\}$, rather than the printed $\{\varnothing,\mathcal F\}$. With literal $U_n$, the next increment is already known at time $n$, so $\mathbb E[X_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]=X_{n+1}$; the nontrivial update differs from $X_n$. Thus the stated martingale claim is false with that indexing. The following uses the standard <Pólya urn> described in words.

Put $m=B_0+R_0$, so the total count is $m+n$. Conditional on $\mathcal F_n$, the next blue indicator $I_{n+1}$ has expectation $X_n$. Hence
$$
\mathbb E[X_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]
=\frac{B_n+\mathbb E[I_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]}{m+n+1}
=\frac{B_n+B_n/(m+n)}{m+n+1}=X_n.
$$
Adaptedness and integrability follow from the recursive construction and $0\leq X_n\leq1$. Thus $X_n$ is a bounded <martingale>. The <martingale convergence theorem> gives an almost-sure limit $X_\infty\in[0,1]$. Boundedness and the <dominated convergence theorem> then give $\mathbb E|X_n-X_\infty|^2\to0$, establishing convergence in $L^2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The blue count never decreases, so $X_n\geq B_0/(m+n)$. For all sufficiently large $n$, this is at least $B_0/[2(n+1)]$. Whenever the fresh draw satisfies $U_{n+1}\leq B_0/[2(n+1)]$, a blue ball is added. Part (a), with $\lambda=B_0/2$, says that such events occur infinitely often almost surely. Therefore $B_n\to\infty$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{nX_n=\frac n{m+n}B_n\longrightarrow\infty\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Since $X_n>0$, the pointwise inequality
$$
\mathbf1_{\{X_\infty=0\}}\leq\frac{(X_n-X_\infty)^2}{X_n^2}
$$
holds: on that event both sides equal one, and elsewhere the left side is zero. Take <conditional expectation> and pull out the positive $\mathcal F_n$-measurable denominator to obtain the requested bound. The supplied variance estimate improves it to
$$
0\leq\mathbb P(X_\infty=0\mid\mathcal F_n)
\leq\frac1{(m+n)X_n}=\frac1{B_n}\longrightarrow0
$$
almost surely, by part (ii). These conditional probabilities are bounded by one. The <dominated convergence theorem> and <tower property of conditional expectation> give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_\infty=0)=\lim_n\mathbb E[\mathbb P(X_\infty=0\mid\mathcal F_n)]
=\mathbb E[\lim_n\mathbb P(X_\infty=0\mid\mathcal F_n)]=0.}
$$
This proves <positivity of the limiting Pólya urn proportion> without needing its explicit beta distribution.

= B
{parent=2010}
